From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-agent 978: tearing down one interface must not delete its whole AP MLD

teardown_iface() runs per interface. The block this patch removes did not:
given any one interface it deleted the entire MLD that interface belonged to -
the mapagent mld section, the wireless mld section, and the mld_id of every
other ap section affiliated with the same MLD. An AP MLD carries up to three
links, so tearing one link down took all three records with it.

Measured on hardware 2026-07-31. Flashing the controller reproduced it on the
first try; a watchdog snapshot taken every second across the boot shows the
window exactly:

  up=9s     mld=2 ap=8 mld_id=5     configuration complete
  up=33s    mld=0 ap=7 mld_id=0     gone
  up=34s    mld=0 ap=6 mld_id=0
  up=181s   mld=2 ap=8 mld_id=5     watchdog put it back -> repairs=1

and the process list captured in that same second names the caller:

  10173 root  {multiap} /bin/sh /lib/wifi/multiap teardown_iface wlan1

reached from wifi_teardown_iface() (agent_map.c:1685) by way of
wifi_teardown_map_ifaces_by_radio(), which the AP-Autoconfiguration branch calls
at agent_map.c:2724, 2747 and 2763. That is why it only shows up after a flash -
a fresh upgrade runs a full autoconfiguration cycle - and why it shows up on the
controller, which runs a colocated agent and configures itself. Flashing either
plain agent in the same session left the configuration untouched, repairs=0.

The sections are created by map_genconfig through "multiap conf". Traced on the
wire in the same boot:

  multiap <- S10boot :: uci set wireless.mld0=wifi-mld
  multiap <- S10boot :: uci set wireless.ap_mld_1=wifi-iface

so the branch that creates them owns them, and a per-interface teardown is not
that branch. This is the same correction as 0268: fix ownership rather than add
a predicate in front of shared state.

It is also a failure we already carry a fix for from the other end. When the mld
sections go missing the agent finds a->bstamld NULL and enters a crash loop, which
is what 997 addresses. Deleting them here produces that state deliberately.

What the interface does own is its own affiliation, and that is still removed:
mapagent_teardown_wireless() drops wireless.<section>.mld for this ifname, and the
BSS itself is disabled just above. What survives is the MLD record.

How to tell this is wrong: after flashing the controller, mld must stay at 2 for
the whole boot - mld-watch records it second by second - and repairs must be 0.
If instead the agent starts crashing, or an MLD is left with no affiliated links,
then the deletion was load-bearing and the fix belongs on the creating side.

Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>

--- a/src/script/multiap	2026-07-31 10:16:04.905343818 +0200
+++ b/src/script/multiap	2026-07-31 10:16:04.905553628 +0200
@@ -815,11 +815,30 @@
 		config_get mld_id $section mld_id
 		uci -q set mapagent.${section}.enabled="0"
 
-		[ "$mld_id" != "" ] && {
-			config_foreach mapagent_teardown_mld mld $mld_id
-			(mapagent_teardown_wireless_mld mld${mld_id})
-		}
-
+		# The MLD sections are not ours to delete.
+		#
+		# This runs per interface, but the block that used to be here
+		# deleted the whole MLD the interface belonged to - the mapagent
+		# mld section, the wireless mld section and the mld_id of every
+		# other ap section affiliated with it. An AP MLD carries up to
+		# three links; tearing one of them down took all three records
+		# with it.
+		#
+		# The sections are created by map_genconfig through
+		# "multiap conf", measured 2026-07-31 on the wire:
+		#
+		#   multiap <- S10boot :: uci set wireless.mld0=wifi-mld
+		#   multiap <- S10boot :: uci set wireless.ap_mld_1=wifi-iface
+		#
+		# so the branch that creates them owns them. Deleting them from
+		# here is also the exact shape of a failure we already carry a
+		# fix for: when the mld sections go missing the agent finds
+		# a->bstamld NULL and enters a crash loop (997).
+		#
+		# What the interface does own is its own affiliation, and that
+		# is still removed below - mapagent_teardown_wireless() drops
+		# wireless.<section>.mld for this ifname. The BSS is disabled
+		# above. What survives is the MLD record itself.
 		(mapagent_teardown_wireless $ifname)
 	}
 
